1
00:00:00,340 --> 00:00:05,185
Imagine you have just opened a training
repository and want to know how its order

2
00:00:05,185 --> 00:00:06,142
validator works.

3
00:00:06,322 --> 00:00:09,524
We ask a short question without attaching
any code.

4
00:00:09,704 --> 00:00:14,949
A language model can produce a plausible
answer based on patterns it has learned,

5
00:00:14,949 --> 00:00:18,834
but the question alone does not identify
the implementation.

6
00:00:19,014 --> 00:00:24,091
Watch how that changes when we supply the
exact function we want to understand.

7
00:00:25,548 --> 00:00:31,209
Before the model processes our request, a
tokenizer converts text into pieces called

8
00:00:31,209 --> 00:00:31,680
tokens.

9
00:00:31,860 --> 00:00:37,139
Code, punctuation, spaces, and different
languages can split in different ways.

10
00:00:37,319 --> 00:00:40,490
The blocks on this screen are a sketch to
show the idea.

11
00:00:40,670 --> 00:00:46,226
Use the tokenizer or usage readout for the
actual model if you need precise counts.

12
00:00:46,406 --> 00:00:48,754
A word count is only an estimate.

13
00:00:50,215 --> 00:00:55,268
The window holds material such as your
prompt, selected code, and conversation

14
00:00:55,268 --> 00:00:58,119
history that the product chooses to
include.

15
00:00:58,299 --> 00:01:02,332
Outputs and other limits also depend on
the model and product.

16
00:01:02,512 --> 00:01:06,852
On screen, the useful material is the
issue and two small files.

17
00:01:07,032 --> 00:01:12,049
Attaching a sprawling log might crowd out
relevant material or add noise to the

18
00:01:12,049 --> 00:01:12,494
answer.

19
00:01:13,965 --> 00:01:17,698
Now we attach the small function and ask
about two inputs.

20
00:01:17,878 --> 00:01:23,108
The comparison checks whether the quantity
is at least zero, so both zero and one

21
00:01:23,108 --> 00:01:23,883
return true.

22
00:01:24,063 --> 00:01:28,781
The function may be wrong for the business
rule, even though we can explain precisely

23
00:01:28,781 --> 00:01:29,502
what it does.

24
00:01:29,682 --> 00:01:34,479
Notice the distinction between observing
the code and deciding what the code should

25
00:01:34,479 --> 00:01:34,652
do.

26
00:01:36,132 --> 00:01:39,227
The assistant has identified the current
behavior.

27
00:01:39,407 --> 00:01:43,279
It still needs the requirement to decide
whether zero should pass.

28
00:01:43,459 --> 00:01:46,692
The issue says an order must have at least
one item.

29
00:01:46,872 --> 00:01:51,033
Now we have two distinct pieces of
evidence, the code and the rule.

30
00:01:51,213 --> 00:01:56,615
A helpful response names the mismatch and
asks for clarification when the relevant

31
00:01:56,615 --> 00:01:57,932
rule is unavailable.

32
00:01:59,382 --> 00:02:00,847
Try this on screen.

33
00:02:01,027 --> 00:02:04,697
In the first chat, attach just the source
function and the issue.

34
00:02:04,877 --> 00:02:10,336
In the second, include the same evidence
along with several unrelated sample notes.

35
00:02:10,516 --> 00:02:13,080
We can inspect which source the answer
uses.

36
00:02:13,260 --> 00:02:18,524
A larger window allows more material, but
our task still benefits from relevant

37
00:02:18,524 --> 00:02:20,524
context and a narrow question.

38
00:02:20,704 --> 00:02:22,540
Check what each response cites.

39
00:02:24,007 --> 00:02:26,495
Finish by running a small boundary check.

40
00:02:26,675 --> 00:02:31,636
Here the original validator returns true
for zero and false for negative one.

41
00:02:31,816 --> 00:02:34,678
That output confirms what we read in the
function.

42
00:02:34,858 --> 00:02:40,131
In your own exercise, attach the smallest
relevant code sample, ask for a reason

43
00:02:40,131 --> 00:02:42,900
tied to a line, and verify the prediction.

44
00:02:43,080 --> 00:02:45,429
Keep the test output as the evidence.
